# Linear sync
Every commit on a story branch (`{STORY_ID}/...`, e.g. `TOK-6/s01-...`) must be
recorded on the matching Linear issue as a comment. The branch's story id is
the first `[A-Z]+-[0-9]+` token in the branch name.

## Contract — after `git commit` on a story branch
For each new commit, in chronological order:
1. Resolve the issue id from the branch name.
2. Call the `plugin-linear-linear.save_comment` MCP tool with:
   - `issueId`: the resolved id (e.g. `TOK-6`).
   - `body`: a short markdown comment containing
     - back-ticked short hash + subject on the first line,
     - a one-line `Scope:` summary of files / top-level dirs touched,
     - a one-line `Verify:` summary (typecheck / lint / test status if relevant),
     - optionally a longer paragraph for context.
3. Append the short hash on its own line to `.cursor/linear-synced.txt`. The
   stop-hook (`linear-sync-prompt.sh`) reads this ledger to know what's done.

Do not skip step 3 — without it the stop hook will keep nudging.

## Enforcement
`linear-sync-prompt.sh` runs on every `stop` event. It computes
`git log <base>..HEAD` (base = `@{upstream}` → `origin/main` → `main`) and
compares short hashes against the ledger. Unsynced commits trigger a
`followup_message` listing them. The hook never calls Linear directly — only
the agent does, via the MCP — so no API token is required in the env.

## Notes
- One comment per commit is the default. Squash multi-commit comments only when
  the commits land in a single push and share narrative.
- If the branch has no story id (e.g. `main`, `chore/bump-deps`), the hook is
  a no-op.
- The ledger is gitignored: it represents per-clone sync state, not project
  state.
